---
// Fig. for "Multivariate & Multilinear Polynomials": a teaser for the
// sumcheck protocol — peeling one variable off a claimed sum at a time,
// using the same f(x,y) = 3 + 2x + 5y + 4xy from the table-to-polynomial
// figure, whose hypercube sum is 3 + 5 + 8 + 1 = 17 = 4 in F(13).
---
<svg viewBox="0 0 760 400" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;">
  <text x="380" y="26" text-anchor="middle" fill="#e9eef5" font-size="16" font-weight="800" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">preview: peeling off one variable</text>

  <rect x="40" y="46" width="680" height="42" rx="10" fill="#111c2e" stroke="#3ab7ff" stroke-width="1.5" />
  <text x="380" y="72" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fdcff" font-size="13" font-weight="700" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">Can a prover convince a verifier of a sum without the verifier adding it up?</text>

  <text x="380" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">CLAIM&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#e9eef5" font-size="13" font-weight="700">sum over x, y in {"{0,1}"} of f(x, y) = 4</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="154" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">ROUND 1&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#8fdcff" font-size="13" font-weight="700">prover sends g&#8321;(x) = f(x,0) + f(x,1) = 11 + 8x, keeping x free</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="186" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">CHECK&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#5ddc8a" font-size="13" font-weight="700">g&#8321;(0) + g&#8321;(1) = 11 + 19 = 4, matches the claim &#10003;</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="218" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11" font-weight="700">CHALLENGE&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#e9eef5" font-size="13" font-weight="700">verifier picks a random r = 2, prover continues at x = 2</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">ROUND 2&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#5ddc8a" font-size="13" font-weight="700">new claim is g&#8321;(2) = 1, and only y is left to pin down</tspan>
  </text>

  <rect x="60" y="272" width="640" height="38" rx="10" fill="#0e1f16" stroke="#5ddc8a" stroke-width="1.8" />
  <text x="380" y="296" text-anchor="middle" fill="#5ddc8a" font-size="13" font-weight="700" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">two variables, two rounds, one small check each &#10003;</text>

  <line x1="40" y1="326" x2="720" y2="326" stroke="#223049" stroke-width="1" />
  <text x="380" y="350" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fdcff" font-size="15" font-weight="800" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">N VARIABLES, N ROUNDS, ONE VARIABLE PEELED EACH TIME</text>
  <text x="380" y="372" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8b96a8" font-size="12" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">This full protocol &#8212; the sumcheck &#8212; is coming up next in the series.</text>
</svg>
